From 0de6b528bf9b11c9051fe8d5acbcbe7b88148925 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 06:05:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 106/112] EQ: bass and treble as real shelving filters, their
 gains and corners moving per sample

v17's EQ (1.2.1's doEQ()) was two forward-Euler one-poles with truncating products and wrapping sums, gains and
corners set once per block, a band's state left stale while it was off, and a treble corner that went past c = 1
(the band stopped doing anything) above about 5.5 kHz. Now (dsp/eq_shelves.h):
- TPT one-pole shelves with a prewarped corner, treble (high shelf) then bass (low shelf) in series; the corners at
  the knobs' centre are v17's -3 dB points (198.9 Hz, 2805.3 Hz), +-6 octaves as before, clamped to 2 Hz..20 kHz,
  gliding in log frequency (10 ms, per block) with G moving per sample.
- The gains exactly v17's for every stored value (g = 4 p^2, up to +12 dB), full cut -60 dB instead of -inf; they
  move per sample in linear amplitude, exactly onto the target (GainRamp: > 6 dB in at least 5 ms).
- The state 32 bits below the LSB (smmlar + 2 smlal per sample): no dead band, no DC bias (v17 at 3 Hz / +12 dB:
  error -77 dBFS, DC -1592 LSB; now -141 dBFS, 0); the output products rounded, every sum saturating.
- A band at its centre takes no time and passes the audio bit-exact, as before; switched on it starts from its
  input with the gain from unity (a fade from dry), switched off it ramps back to unity before stopping.
CPU (Cortex-A9 emulator, 128 samples): both bands 16.4k instructions (v17 8.1k), one band 7.4k, off 62.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Eb214cM1zi1k5Gpcn9RLKh
